,"n":133,"first":1892,"last":2025,"intercept":107.19326647822405,"slope":-0.045720334887734825,"total":-6.1},"snow_normal":16.8},"titles":{"year":"2026 so far: 19 daily record highs, no daily record lows","records":"Amarillo is setting record highs far faster than chance","change":"Amarillo's nights have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 58\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>58\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 62\u00b0F, and the record is 82\u00b0F (2025). The low of <strong>38\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Amarillo is setting record highs far faster than chance","p_rec":"If Amarillo's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Amarillo has set <strong>45 standing record lows</strong>, fewer than the 73 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">182 record highs</strong>, far more than the 73 that chance alone would give. The 2020s hold the most record highs of any decade (93). Amarillo's all-time high is 111\u00b0F, on June 26, 2011.","t_chg":"Amarillo's nights have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","p_chg":"Across 134 complete years: Amarillo's average low shows no clear change: +0.02\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.04 to +0.08) that includes zero. Amarillo's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.31\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.23 to +0.39), about +4.1\u00b0F since 1892. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.70\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.44 to +0.96) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+1.14\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.78 to +1.50). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 2 of 12 months. Highs rose in 11 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Amarillo's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.17 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 19.6 inches a year. The wettest year was 1923 (39.75 in); the driest, 2011 (7.01 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 19 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}